What is ObjectLevelAccessControl

    This is a situation when a user with username John try to call /user/get-user-info on different username
    Example : John will call /user/get-user-info/Jane
    This should not be allowed.

What Happens Under the Hood?

    By default, when you use @WebMvcTest, Spring Boot only loads a tiny, minimal slice of your application (the controller classes and your global web configurations).
    To keep your tests running fast, Spring Boot turns off its deep security inspection features by default.
    Even though your controller has @PreAuthorize written on it, the test container treats it like a normal, plain text comment. It doesn't look at it or enforce it unless you explicitly force it to.

The Two Special Additions Explained

1. Why do we need @EnableMethodSecurity?

    Think of this annotation as the Master Switch.
    By default, Spring Security only looks at URLs (like /admin/** or /user/**).
    It does not look inside your Java methods to inspect annotations like @PreAuthorize.
    Adding @EnableMethodSecurity turns on a powerful engine called Spring AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming).
    This engine actively scans your methods right before they run to check if the caller meets the criteria.

2. Why do we need @Import(AuthorizationTest.MethodSecurityConfig.class)?

    If you put @EnableMethodSecurity directly inside your test class without wrapping it up in its own standalone nested class configuration block, Spring Boot gets confused and turns off its component scanner.
    By making a dedicated container (public static class MethodSecurityConfig {}) and passing it into the class-level @Import, you are saying: "Spring, please keep loading all my controllers normally, but cleanly plug in this extra Method Security Master Switch on top of them."


The Restaurant Analogy

    Let us bring back our busy high-end restaurant to see exactly how this operates in a real-life environment.
        • The SecurityFilterChain: The security guard standing at the front door checking IDs.
        • The Controller Method: The Head Chef's private recipe locker inside the kitchen.
        • @PreAuthorize: A strict biometric fingerprint scanner installed directly onto the handle of the recipe locker.

        Scenario A: Before the Fix (Missing the Switch)

        1. John walks into the restaurant. The front door guard (SecurityFilterChain) looks at his ID. John is a registered customer, so the guard lets him walk into the customer zone (/user/**).
        2. John walks all the way into the kitchen, finds the private locker belonging to Jane (/user/get-user-info/Jane), and reaches for the handle.
        3. Because the restaurant manager forgot to flip the master switch to power up the lock (missing @EnableMethodSecurity), the fingerprint scanner (@PreAuthorize) is turned off! The screen is pitch black.
        4. John opens the locker with no resistance and reads Jane's private files. The restaurant incorrectly delivers a 200 OK response.

        Scenario B: After the Fix (The Switch is Powered On)

        1. You installed a dedicated backup battery line (@Import(MethodSecurityConfig.class)) and flipped the main power breaker switch (@EnableMethodSecurity).
        2. John walks past the front door guard safely.
        3. He sneaks up to Jane's private recipe locker and touches the handle.
        4. BZZZ! The powered-on fingerprint scanner (@PreAuthorize) instantly turns on. It reads John's print, checks the system logs, and shouts: "Hey! You are John, but you are trying to open Jane's locker! And you don't have a Manager master key badge!"
        5. The alarm sounds, the security system locks down, and John is thrown out of the kitchen with a strict 403 Forbidden!